@meta
id: 17-14
title: Delete to the character, not the line
chapter: 17
type: lesson
skills: marks-set, mark-backtick
par: 28
minutes: 5
vscode: click before LATE, shift-click before OTHER, Delete
open: refund.reasons.ts

@brief
Seven refund reasons are being retired: everything from `'LATE'` up to
and including `'NOT_AS_DESCRIBED'`. `'DAMAGED'`, `'WRONG_ITEM'`,
`'MISSING_PART'` and `'OTHER'` stay, and the labels below them stay
too.

The run you are deleting starts in the middle of one line and ends in
the middle of another, two lines later, so it is not a set of whole
lines and no text object describes it. It is a span between two
characters. Mark one end, go to the other, delete to the mark, and
make sure you use the form of the mark that means the character.

The result should be one line:

    'DAMAGED', 'WRONG_ITEM', 'MISSING_PART', 'OTHER',

@keys
| `ma` | mark the character under the cursor |
| `` d`a `` | delete from here to the marked character, not including it |
| `d'a` | delete whole lines from here to the marked line; wrong here |
| `?text` | search backwards |

@hints
Search for `'OTHER` and the cursor lands on the quote before it. Mark
that. Then search back to the quote before `LATE`.
--
`` d`a `` is charwise and exclusive: it deletes everything from the
cursor up to but not including the mark. That is exactly why marking
the quote before `OTHER` keeps `'OTHER'` intact.
--
`/'OTHER` Enter, `ma`, `?'LATE` Enter, `` d`a ``.

@solution
    /'OTHER<Enter> ma ?'LATE<Enter> d`a :wq<Enter>

This is the one situation where the backtick is not a convenience but
the whole answer. `d'a` would delete three complete lines, taking
`'DAMAGED'` and `'OTHER'` with them. `` d`a `` deletes a span of
characters that happens to cross two line breaks, and leaves both ends
of the lines alone.

The exclusive rule is the thing to internalise: a charwise motion to a
mark stops just before the mark. So put the mark on the first thing
you want to *keep*, and delete up to it. It is the same rule as `dt)`
and `d/pattern`, and once you see it as the same rule it stops being a
surprise.

Everything here generalises: `` y`a `` yanks the span, `` c`a ``
replaces it, `` >`a `` indents the lines it touches. The mark is just a
motion, and a charwise one when you spell it with a backtick.

@start refund.reasons.ts
export const REFUND_REASONS = [
  'DAMAGED', 'WRONG_ITEM', 'MISSING_PART', 'LATE',
  'DUPLICATE_ORDER', 'CHANGED_MIND', 'PRICE_MATCH',
  'FRAUD_SUSPECTED', 'NOT_AS_DESCRIBED', 'OTHER',
] as const;

export type RefundReason = (typeof REFUND_REASONS)[number];

export const refundReasonLabels: Record<string, string> = {
  DAMAGED: 'Arrived damaged',
  WRONG_ITEM: 'Wrong item sent',
  MISSING_PART: 'Part missing from the box',
  OTHER: 'Other',
};

@expected refund.reasons.ts
export const REFUND_REASONS = [
  'DAMAGED', 'WRONG_ITEM', 'MISSING_PART', 'OTHER',
] as const;

export type RefundReason = (typeof REFUND_REASONS)[number];

export const refundReasonLabels: Record<string, string> = {
  DAMAGED: 'Arrived damaged',
  WRONG_ITEM: 'Wrong item sent',
  MISSING_PART: 'Part missing from the box',
  OTHER: 'Other',
};
